      <floats-group id="article-27029.s17">
        <fig id="article-27029.image.f3" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Instruments for Peritonsillar Abscess Management. Left to right: 10 mL control syringe and 18-gauge spinal needle with 15 mm of needle exposed after the guard was cut back, Schnidt tonsil clamp (or Kelly clamp), and Weider sweetheart retractor (or tongue depressors). Leaving 15 mm of needle exposed helps to prevent inadvertent injury to the internal carotid artery. A #15 blade scalpel is at the top; a longer #7 Bard-Parker handle may be used with the same blade if available. Contributed by MH Hohman, MD, FACS</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="IMG_7670" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
